term Rasterization {
  id: "@community/term-rasterization"
  version: "1.0.0"

  meaning: "The process by which vector graphics, font glyphs, and CSS-defined shapes are converted into a pixel grid for display. Rasterization happens inside the GPU/CPU rendering pipeline of the browser and determines whether edges appear sharp, soft, or aliased on the user's screen."

  domain: visual-design

  aliases: ["rastering", "scan conversion", "rendering to pixels"]

  notes: [
    "Subpixel rendering uses the RGB stripes inside each LCD pixel for ~3x horizontal resolution on text. Effective on landscape LCDs; less or zero benefit on Retina/HiDPI displays.",
    "CSS property `text-rendering: optimizeLegibility` enables advanced kerning and ligatures at small sizes; `geometricPrecision` rasterizes vectors with fidelity over speed. `auto` is the browser default.",
    "`-webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased` and `-moz-osx-font-smoothing: grayscale` switch from subpixel to grayscale antialiasing on macOS — common in dark-mode UIs to avoid 'fuzzy' bold weights, but reduces small-text crispness.",
    "Subpixel positioning is critical for animation smoothness: `transform: translateX(0.5px)` on Retina is rasterized at the half-pixel boundary, while `left: 0.5px` typically snaps to whole pixels.",
    "GPU rasterization (browser layer compositing) is faster but introduces aliasing artifacts on rotated/scaled content. Use `will-change: transform` only when measurable performance benefit is observed, and remove it after the animation.",
    "DPR (devicePixelRatio) determines how many physical pixels render one CSS pixel: 1× on standard displays, 2×/3× on Retina/HiDPI. Image assets should ship 2× variants via srcset to avoid blurry rasterization on HiDPI.",
    "SVG rasterization at draw time produces crisp results at any zoom level — preferred over raster (PNG/JPG) for icons, logos, and UI illustrations.",
  ]

  related: [
    @community/term-leading,
    @community/transform-px-to-rem,
    @community/check-image-optimization,
  ]
}
